<p>As usual, <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/11/hello-everyone-its-john-apples-q4-2026-earnings-call-transcript/">Snell also has a transcript</a> of <a href="https://www.apple.com/investor/earnings-call/">the analyst call</a> — Ternus’s first as CEO. My favorite tidbit was his line at the end of this answer:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p><strong>Richard Kramer, Arete Research:</strong> Your latest MacBook Pros
included more iOS features like Touch and Dynamic Island as well
as, obviously, Siri AI. Should we anticipate further close linking
of macOS and iOS features, maybe even pointing at some point to a
single unified OS, either for simplicity or to reduce costs?</p>

<p><strong>John Ternus:</strong> Look, we are very proud of both the Mac and the
iPad, and I think what you’ve seen over the years is we’ve really
enjoyed the ability to bring features and capabilities across the
ecosystem and create really compelling features that span things
between handoff between iPhone and Mac and iPad, and so when we
design the product, we’re looking at how can we make the best
version of each and every product, and if you look at what we just
did last week with the MacBook Pro, we took a huge leap forward.
These MacBook Pros are better in every way than the ones that came
before them, and we’re really proud of that, and so we don’t have
any fundamentally different changes that we’re planning right now,
nor would we talk about it anyway.</p>
</blockquote>

